# Spire Consoles Conventions

## Architecture conformance (required)
- All implementations must conform to `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (module boundaries, layered architecture, and conventions).
- In particular: keep UI in `views/`, view-logic in `*UseCase` classes, API integration in `service/`, and shared UI building blocks in `components/`.

## Follow existing patterns first (required)
- Before adding any feature, class, method, or component, search the codebase for similar implementations.
- If an established pattern, structure, naming convention, or architectural approach already exists, conform to it unless the user explicitly requests otherwise.
- Reuse design patterns, package/folder structure, naming, coding style, logging, error handling, validation, and API design already used in the project.
- Prefer extending or adapting existing code over introducing new patterns.
- If multiple patterns exist or the codebase is inconsistent, call that out and ask which approach to follow before proceeding.

## API paths in services
- Define one `BASE_PATH` per service (e.g. `"/api/v1/spire/demarcations"`).
- Derive all related endpoints by composing from `BASE_PATH` (avoid duplicating the prefix literal).
- If a new endpoint shares an existing base, prefer `BASE_PATH + "/..."` over a second hard-coded string.

## DTO / model usage
- Prefer using Spire API model types directly (or via Kotlin `typealias`) when they match the needed shape.
- Introduce console-local DTOs only when:
  - the UI needs a simplified/derived shape (e.g. hierarchy breadcrumb items), or
  - the API lacks an appropriate model for the response being consumed, or
  - the console needs a stable UI contract decoupled from API changes.

## UI detail cards & hierarchy
- Use `DetailFieldsCard` for dynamic form-like detail rendering from `List<AField>`, `List<List<AField>>`, or simple `Map` empty-states.
- Don’t force non-form layouts (e.g. hierarchy breadcrumb/chips with separators and “current” styling) into `DetailFieldsCard`.
- If reuse is desired, extract a shared “detail card wrapper/header builder” rather than bending form builders to custom layouts.

## Learning workflow → rules
- When the user repeatedly corrects the same kind of issue or follows a consistent workflow, treat it as a candidate for a Cursor rule.
- Proactively suggest adding/updating a rule when you notice a pattern more than once.
